     3	The ferry came in on the two hundred and seventy-seventh day past the Line, at first light, with the river running high and brown under it and the Line's lead on the sack at its bow, and he was on the landing to see it because he catalogued arrivals, and Seln was beside him because Seln read faces off boats.
     4	
     5	He catalogued the ferry first, because it was the first of the year and he wanted the count. The same flat hull, tarred over the winter and smelling of it. The same rope, new-spliced where the ice had frayed it, singing in the ferryman's hands. The boy at the stern pole. Hides, six bales, a broker's mark on four of them. The tarred sack, the lead on it, the season's first mail, which would have in it whatever the Line had decided to say to the edge over a winter of not being able to. And one passenger, at the bow, standing, with a pack at his feet and a stick in his hand, who was not a carter and not a broker's man and was looking at the landing the way a man looks at a place he has read about in a file.
     6	
     7	Cael knew the stance before he knew the face. He had stood in front of that stance in a hall at sixteen, and at a table at seventeen, and in a borrowed room at eighteen with nothing anywhere deciding what he was, and a body learns a stance the way it learns a stair. Feet apart. Weight down. The hands still. A man built to hold a line, standing at the bow of a rope ferry in a coat that had never been grey, holding a stick.
