   119	Cael catalogued that, because it was the thing at the table he had not had a catalogue for. The man had brought a problem to a table, which he had done all his life, and had set it down, which he had done all his life, and then the part that came next did not come, because the part that came next had always been his — the team, the warrant, the order given, the response owned before the problem was fully spoken — and there was no team and no warrant and nobody at this table was going to wait for him to give an order, and he sat with his hands flat on the wood and his stick against the table's edge and watched Karis own the response, because Karis was already owning it: the notebook, the pencil, the three questions she was going to ask in the order she had them. He watched her as a man watches a thing he used to do being done by somebody else. It was not a small thing to watch. Cael had seen it once before, on a bluff, in a man who had just been told what his own file said; he had not expected to see it in this one.
   120	
   121	"Why us," Lira said.
   122	
   123	It was the first thing she had said. She had sat down last — after Seln, after Oryn, after Brom, who had sat when Vastin sat because Brom sat when a man he was going to have to price sat — and she had sat at the far end, as far from the brown coat as the table allowed, with her two arms on the wood, and she asked it flat, the way she asked the honest question.
   124	
   125	"Because I am not going to take it to a council," Vastin said, "and I will tell you why in a moment, when the question is asked properly, because I think it is going to be. But the short answer to yours is arithmetic. The Compact spent four years trying to file this boy. It could not. I know, because I was the one who tried. Everyone else on the continent who has read what I have read is inside the Compact, and everyone inside the Compact who reads a thing like this buries it, because burying it is the job, and I know that too, because that was also me." He looked down the table at Cael, once, and away. "The only people I could think of who would do anything with a name and a locked layer but bury them were the six people the Compact could not file. So I came to them. It was not a difficult piece of reasoning. It was only a long walk."
   126	
   127	Oryn said, then, to nobody, in the voice for a finding: "That knee's carried him three hundred miles and he hasn't asked me for anything."
   128	
   129	"I didn't know you were a healer."
   130	
   131	"You do now."
   132	
   133	"Then I still haven't asked."
   134	
   135	Oryn looked at him a moment longer, and nodded, once, as if a thing had been entered, and looked away.
   136	
   137	"What follows you," Lira said.
   138	
   139	"Nothing that can cross the Line." He said it as fact, in the ledger voice, the one thing at the table he was still certain of. "The restriction on him is pending jurisdiction. There is no jurisdiction here. There is no one behind me and no one after me, and if there were, they would not be after me — I am a man who resigned, and men who resign are filed, not followed. What follows this boy is a piece of paper that stops at a river." A pause. "What followed you to the Stair in the autumn is another matter, and it is not mine, and I do not know whose it is, and I would give a great deal to."
   140	
   141	Cael had not said anything. He had not said anything since the plank, and the five of them had let him not say it, because the man at the end of the table had come to speak to six and had spoken to five, and the sixth was the one all of it was about, and everyone at the table knew that the sixth would say what he said when he had it.
   142	
   143	He had it. He had had it since the landing. He remembered a table in a borrowed office in Ostrand, four streets back from the registry seat, a room with nothing of anyone's in it, and the man in front of him now in grey, and a sentence, said once, after the longest silence he had ever sat through: *I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be.* And then nothing. No alliance, no files, no hand across the table. The man had said the sentence and gone back inside and stayed there, and Cael had carried the sentence across the Line in Hesk's satchel as the only true thing the Compact had ever handed him, and had never once expected to hear the voice that said it again.
   144	
   145	He did not say any of that. He said, to Karis: "Ask him the thing you want to ask him."
   146	
   147	Karis looked at him. Then she put the pencil down, and put her hands flat on the notebook, and asked it plainly, because she was the only one at the table who would ask it plainly, and the table had been waiting for her to.
   148	
   149	"Why did you bring this to us," Karis said, "and not to a council, or a court?"
   150	
   151	"Because a council would file it," Vastin said, "and I spent forty years being the man they sent to make sure the filing held. I know exactly how deep a thing has to be buried before no one goes looking. This is deeper than that." He was quiet a moment. His hands did not move on the table. "I helped bury smaller things. I'd like to dig one up before I'm done."
   152	
   153	Nobody answered him.
   154	
   155	Behind him, through the road door that Pike kept a hand's width open for the smoke, the ferry pushed off from the landing with the season's first sack going west, and the rope sang once in the ferryman's hands, and the boat turned in the brown water, and went.

