   107	And Seln.
   108	
   109	Seln had gone still.
   110	
   111	Not the stillness he used on a road, which was a thing he did on purpose, so that a body that had been moving stopped being a thing anyone could see. The other one. The one Cael had seen twice in his life: once at a plank in a town on no map when a completion report had been read aloud to him, and once on a rim in the dark with four crossbows in the scrub and no faces. Seln, at the door end, with his shoulder on the wall, had heard a date and a word and had gone still in the way a professional goes still when he has just done a sum in his head and does not like the answer, and he did not say the answer. He did not say anything. He looked at the sheet in Karis's hand, and then at the bar of ink on it, and then — once, no longer — at Cael, and then at the window, and he did not say it.
   112	
   113	Cael did not know what it was.
   114	
   115	He knew Seln had a sum, and had done it, and had the answer, and had decided at the door end of a back room not to say it aloud, and he knew that Seln had made that decision before about a thing in a margin and had been right to. He did not know the sum. He wrote down, in the part of him that wrote things down, that Seln had gone still at a date and a bar of ink, and that he had seen it, and that he did not know why, and he let it stand, because a thing you do not know is a thing you do not know, and the log had a place for those.
   116	
   117	Karis copied the line. She copied it into the notebook with the date in it and the word *correlated* and then the bar, which she drew as a bar, the width of a thumb, and under it, in her own hand, smaller, two words: *not read*.
   118	
   119	---
   120	

