   141	Cael wrote his letter that afternoon, at the meal-sack end of the deal. He had owed it since a wash on the Stair road, to a tall exact person on a bluff who had written that he still lost every time he ran it. For the whole of a cold term he had not known what to say to a friend who was walking a bout through by himself in an empty yard. He knew now, since a girl with grass in her hair had shown him.
   142	
   143	*Ephram. You lose because you run it alone. I lost to a seven-foot pole all the cold term, three to none and then three to one, and each time somebody on a bench told me what I'd done before I had wiped the snow off. Find a bench. Tell Rooke the yard out here is quieter too. He'd not say it was worse. — C.*
   144	
   145	He put no place on it. He folded it, and laid a copper on top, and left the two together inside Hesk's book for the boat.
   146	
   147	It rained that night, for the first time since the snow, and in the morning Lira came to the board with her left arm held in against her and her mouth shut. A healer had told her that it would ache in the wet for a year. She did not speak of it. When Brom wanted the new hurdle lifted into its place between the shed's posts, she took one end of it in both hands before anybody else had stirred, and walked it round the drum with her jaw set, and lashed her end to its post herself.
   148	
   149	By the two hundred and seventy-sixth the water was over the landing's lowest plank. It came down the colour of a ploughed field, with a noise that could be heard in the inn's kitchen, and it brought whole trees with it, and once a drowned sheep, and once the better part of somebody's footbridge. The cable hung over it in a long belly. The ferryman sat on his keg from first light with his arms folded, and for an hour he did nothing but look. Half the meadow stood on the bank and looked at him, for there were nineteen fires along that grass now, and thirty carts with their shafts down.
   150	
