   179	The station stood above the water on the farther side, and he would have known it for one at any distance, since he had inspected such houses for the whole of his service. There was the low stone house with its slate roof, and the bench under the wall, and the board for the day's hours, which the rain had washed clean; and behind the house stood the second building, with no window and a wide door. Somebody had mended the slates with twists of wire. Somebody had swept the floor. There was a heather broom by the hearth, and turf in the basket, and on the counter a book lay open at its ribbon.
   180	
   181	The wide door of the second building stood a hand's breadth open. He went as far as that door, and looked in at a plate lying in the floor under its frame, brown as an old coin, with dust on it. He had put his name for forty years to the protocol that governed every such plate. He had once had a boy stood on one, on a bluff, and watched it stutter and go dark; and when the form came round that would have stood the boy there a second time, he had declined. He had never told the boy why. He drew the door to, as he had found it, and went back into the house.
   182	
   183	He read the page that it was open at, since it was a station's register and he had never in his life walked past one.
   184	
   185	It was ruled in the printed columns that he could have drawn in his sleep. There was the day, and the name, and the age; there was the reading, and beside the reading a narrow column with no heading on it; and last there was *Entered*. The final line had a girl's name in it, and an age, which was fourteen. After the age there was nothing. A keeper's careful hand had come as far as the fourth column, and had not gone into it.
   186	
   187	He knew what belonged in that column. He had certified the instrument that filled it, in every district of the Compact, for longer than that keeper had been a man. He knew as well what the narrow one was for, having read forty of its entries on a scrubbed table in a borrowed room, each with an order clipped behind it.
   188	
   189	His hand went to the corner of the leaf. A register is turned back as a matter of course; an inspecting officer reads a year of it before he so much as asks for the keys.
