   169	---
   170	
   171	Twelve days before that boat, when the count at Lowmarch stood at two hundred and sixty-five, Vastin had come down to the last ford on the east road with a stick in his hand. He had never heard of the count.
   172	
   173	It was the day on which five people waded a river in flood to reach a healer's gate, a long way to the east of him. He knew nothing of that. He knew that his knee had begun to complain at about the hundredth mile and had left off being civil at the two hundredth. The water before him was cold, and came to the top of his boots, and would have to be walked through.
   174	
   175	He walked through it. On the near shingle a flat boat lay with its bottom stove in, and two black posts leaned beside it. A board had been left face upward in the grass. The paint had gone out of its letters, but they had been cut before they were painted. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* At the head of it was the registry's seal.
   176	
   177	He stood over that board longer than a toll deserved. He had sat once on an inquiry into a ferryman's book, in a district on the coast, and had found for the ferryman. It had not occurred to him then to ask what became of the names.
   178	
   179	The station stood above the water on the farther side, and he would have known it for one at any distance, since he had inspected such houses for the whole of his service. There was the low stone house with its slate roof, and the bench under the wall, and the board for the day's hours, which the rain had washed clean; and behind the house stood the second building, with no window and a wide door. Somebody had mended the slates with twists of wire. Somebody had swept the floor. There was a heather broom by the hearth, and turf in the basket, and on the counter a book lay open at its ribbon.
   180	
   181	The wide door of the second building stood a hand's breadth open. He went as far as that door, and looked in at a plate lying in the floor under its frame, brown as an old coin, with dust on it. He had put his name for forty years to the protocol that governed every such plate. He had once had a boy stood on one, on a bluff, and watched it stutter and go dark; and when the form came round that would have stood the boy there a second time, he had declined. He had never told the boy why. He drew the door to, as he had found it, and went back into the house.
   182	
   183	He read the page that it was open at, since it was a station's register and he had never in his life walked past one.
   184	
   185	It was ruled in the printed columns that he could have drawn in his sleep. There was the day, and the name, and the age; there was the reading, and beside the reading a narrow column with no heading on it; and last there was *Entered*. The final line had a girl's name in it, and an age, which was fourteen. After the age there was nothing. A keeper's careful hand had come as far as the fourth column, and had not gone into it.
   186	
   187	He knew what belonged in that column. He had certified the instrument that filled it, in every district of the Compact, for longer than that keeper had been a man. He knew as well what the narrow one was for, having read forty of its entries on a scrubbed table in a borrowed room, each with an order clipped behind it.
   188	
   189	His hand went to the corner of the leaf. A register is turned back as a matter of course; an inspecting officer reads a year of it before he so much as asks for the keys.
   190	
   191	He was not an inspecting officer. He had left the seal of his office in the tray marked *done*, some months ago, on a morning of rain, with nothing written to go beside it. His clerk had come for that tray at the usual hour. Whether what he had done was a resignation, or only a man going down a stair, was a question for an office to settle, and he had not waited to hear it settled.
   192	
   193	He took his hand away, and left the book at its ribbon.
   194	
   195	Between the house and the windowless building a sill of stone was set in the ground from wall to wall, with a socket at either end for a bar. There had been a keeper here to lift that bar, and an officer, when the district sent one, to stand beside him and put out a hand for whatever a traveller carried. It was the last place on that road where it could be done.
   196	
   197	He was the only man within a hundred miles who had ever been such an officer. He stood at the sill in a drover's coat of brown frieze, which was too short in the sleeve and smelled of sheep; he had bought it at the last market inside the Line, and had left a grey one on its hook. He had an ash stick, cut out of a hedge. He had a pack with one strap mended in cord. Under the pack, flat against his back, lay a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, which he had carried so for three hundred miles less a few. Inside his coat, in a fold of oilcloth, there was a single leaf.
   198	
   199	Nobody asked him for any of it. The wind went over the slates, and a door that had not been latched moved a little on its hinges.
   200	
   201	He stepped over the stone, bad leg first.
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	It took him twelve days to come down to the river, and in the course of them three people told him the same thing.
   206	
   207	He kept to the road, since there was nothing else to keep to. It was a country without a wall or a gate or a board of hours, and for the first two days he met nobody at all. He slept under a pine. He learned to put the bad leg down straight and to let the stick take what the knee would not, and he learned that he could do ten miles so, and not eleven.
   208	
   209	The first was a woman on a mule, at a ford where two rivers ran together. There was a cairn on his bank and another on the far one, and they did not stand opposite. He was looking at that when she rode into the water from the other side and came over on the slant, cairn to cairn, with the stream never above the animal's knee. She was brown and spare, and her hat had the brim cut away on the right. A hatchet hung at her saddle with its edge turned outward.
   210	
   211	"I am looking for five people," said Vastin, "who came east by this road before the last cold. Where would a man ask after them?"
   212	
   213	She looked at him as she might have looked at a letter that somebody held out, to judge what it weighed. Her eyes went to his boots, and his stick, and came up.
   214	
   215	"Go to the plank," she said.
   216	
